HOW TO RUN THIS DISK====================This is a data disk for an OS-9 system: no OS9Boot, nothing to boot from.Give it a disk of its own and let two device names reach it -- /dd, wheremost programs here look for their data, and /h0, which a few of them carryinside -- and mount your own system disk as /h1, where SYS/login looks foryour commands.  That is the whole arrangement, the same for running theprograms and for keeping them (DOC/README-KEEP), and it is described infull under THE ARRANGEMENT below.Getting it onto that disk.  The collection ships as a raw RBF disk image,to be written whole to a disk your system mounts.  We have not done thison real hardware ourselves and say so rather than guess; the image wasmade by os9exec's `mount -k' and carries the geometry in its ID sector.If you have, what worked is worth writing down here.To try the collection without an OS-9 system, os9exec -- a communityemulator that builds on macOS, Linux, Windows and most anything with a Ccompiler -- mounts the image directly: you point it at the disk withOS9DISK and name a program, and that disk becomes /dd.  The commands areunder THE ARRANGEMENT.Most of the programs in CMDS and CMDS/GAMES need nothing but this disk.------------------------------------------------------Trying things before you fetch cio------------------------------------------------------If you run OS-9 you have cio, csl and math -- but the SDK may be on a shelfrather than to hand.  This is what works in the meantime, so you can lookaround the disk straight away:    a shell        sh, or bash    list a dir     ls    read a file    cat, or less    copy a file    cat one > twocp, mv, rm, mkdir and dir are starred -- they are the GNU ones, and theywant cio like the other starred programs.  Under os9exec the emulator hasits own copy, move, rename and makdir, which need nothing loaded at all:    os9exec -r icopy   <from> <to>    os9exec -r imakdir <dir>    os9exec -r move    <from> <to>    os9exec -r rename  <old> <new>Those are commands of the EMULATOR, not programs on the disk.  You cangive them on the line that starts os9exec, as above, OR TYPE THEM AT ASHELL PROMPT INSIDE IT, which is usually what you want: `rename old new'at the disk's own bash does the same job.  On your own hardware theequivalents are the ones you already have: copy, makdir, del, list anddir.The same set will also TELL YOU WHAT THE EMULATOR IS HOLDING, which noprogram on this disk can answer -- there is no `procs' or `mdir' here:    iprocs   the process table: id, state, parent, last system call    imdir    the modules that are loaded    idevs    the devices, and which image or directory each one is    ipaths   the open paths    imem     the memory blocks`os9exec -ih' lists them all.  They are case-sensitive: type `imdir' inlower case, because `MDIR' would look for a program of that name.cio, csl and math now SHIP on this disk, with Microware's permission, sothe starred programs run too and nothing here is special.  DOC/README-CIOhas the detail.The starred ones in DOC/INDEX use Microware's cio (see DOC/README-CIO).They run: the module is here.MAKE YOUR TERMINAL 80 COLUMNS BY 24 LINES----------------------------------------Do this before running anything full-screen.  It is not a preference and itis not something the programs can work around.OS-9 has no call that answers how big your terminal is.  Most full-screenprograms learn their size from the termcap entry for TERM, and the entryhere says 24 lines of 80 columns -- so that is what they believe, whateveryour window is actually set to.THE EXCEPTION IS `less', and anything else that reads the environmentvariables LINES and COLUMNS before the termcap.  `resize' asks the terminalitself -- a VT100-style terminal says where its cursor stops in the bottomright corner -- and at the bash prompt SYS/login gives you it runs as youlog in and sets both.  Drag the window to a new size and type `resize'again.  Measured 2026-09-22: in a 40-line window less puts its prompt online 40.  A terminal that does not answer leaves the 24 by 80 standing.If your window is taller, the programs draw into the top 24 lines and thebottom is dead space -- untidy but harmless.  If it is SHORTER or NARROWER,the screen scrolls where the program does not expect it to and the displayfalls apart.  `life' is the clearest example: correct at 80x24, a mess atanything else.MEASURED, 2026-08-27, so this is not a warning anybody guessed at.  `life' wasdriven on a pseudo-terminal sized 40 columns by 12 lines and captured(tools/playtest.py, `size' directive).  At 80x24 the bottom row reads    Generation: 12                Cycles every 8 generations.and at 40x12 that same status line is written straight THROUGH the board:    Gene@..@@@: 3The mechanism is not scrolling.  `life' addresses its status line to line 24absolutely; a terminal shorter than that clamps the address to its own lastline, which is somewhere in the middle of the picture.  Nothing is lost offthe bottom -- it is overwritten in place, which is worse, because the boardstill looks like a board.AND CHANGING TERM WILL NOT HELP.  /dd/SYS/termcap holds exactly ONE entry:    xterm-256color|xterm|vt100|xterm with 256 colors:            :co#80:it#8:li#24:All three names are aliases for that one description, and it says 80 columnsby 24 lines outright.  So `TERM=vt100', `TERM=xterm' and `TERM=xterm-256color'all tell a program the same thing, whatever your window is really set to.  Terminal.app, iTerm, xterm, a browser terminal:  for the termcap-only  programs -- `life' and most games -- SIZE THE WINDOW TO 80x24 before you  start.  less and its like follow `resize' at any size.  Or, if you would rather keep your window as it is, edit the `co#' and `li#'  numbers in /dd/SYS/termcap to match it.  That is the only thing that  changes what the programs believe.SOME PROGRAMS WANT TERMCAP TO BE THE ENTRY ITSELF-------------------------------------------------SYS/login sets TERMCAP to /dd/SYS/termcap -- the NAME OF THE FILE --and that is what about seventy-five programs here expect.  A handfulwere built against a different termcap library, one that reads TERMCAPas the CAPABILITY STRING itself.  Those get the pathname where theyexpected a terminal description, and stop:    'vt100': Unknown terminal typeIt reads like a broken program and it is not.  At the bash promptSYS/login gives you, the five -- sc, gnuchess, gnuchessn, typefastand yahtzee -- are functions in /dd/.bashrc that hand theprogram the entry for that one run, so they start by name.  Anywhereelse, SYS/termcap.entry exists for exactly this -- source it, and theywork:    . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry    gnuchessMEASURED 2026-08-29: gnuchess and gnuchessn.  GNU Chess drawsits board, keeps both clocks and plays.  Both were recorded as brokenbefore that, on a wrong reading of the message above as wanting aterminfo database.MEASURED AGAIN 2026-09-22, over all 95 termcap programs with TERM andTERMCAP as SYS/login sets them: sc, gnuchess, gnuchessn and typefastanswer `Unknown terminal type' -- and yahtzee, found the next day --and nothing else does -- hexedit, umacs,jargon -m and the rest start.  `sc' is the odd one: with TERM=vt100 itreads the file, and with xterm or xterm-256color, the names SYS/loginuses, it does not.If a full-screen program here stops with `Unknown terminal type',this is the first thing to try.DO NOT set it permanently.  TERMCAP as a PATHNAME is what the otherseventy-odd termcap programs on this disk need, and setting it to theentry breaks them instead.  Source it in the shell where you want oneof the four, and the rest of your session is unaffected.A FEW PROGRAMS NEED A MODULE LOADED FIRST----------------------------------------Most of this disk needs nothing but this disk.  A handful link a libraryMODULE rather than a file, and a module has to be in the module directorybefore it can be linked.  That is what `load' is for -- your own OS-9's,which SYS/login puts on PATH from /h1/CMDS:    load /dd/CMDS/os9libWhat needs it: the RTF Fortran-77 system -- rtf, for, lnk, biory, creadoc --which links `os9lib'.  Without it they print NOTHING and exit, which readslike five broken programs and is not.  DOC/README-FORTRAN has the detail.Verified: with os9lib loaded, `for', `lnk' and `lnk.org' print their commandlines, `rtf' prints "command must be: RTF pathname [options..]", and `biory'prompts for a name and a birth date in German.  With nothing loaded all fiveare silent.  That silence is the whole symptom.The Atari `Graph' library and `VMod_trap' are a different matter and noamount of loading fixes them: both run in supervisor state.  Loaded, theyare found and entered, and the fault moves INTO the library -- a privilegeviolation on its own `RTE', which is a supervisor-only instruction.  Six ofthe seven Graph programs stop at the same address inside it.  DOC/STATUS sayswhich programs those are.AND A FEW FORK ONE OF YOUR OWN UTILITIES----------------------------------------Different thing, same symptom.  This collection carries no Microwareutilities -- they are yours, and they came with your system -- and ahandful of programs here fork one BY NAME in the middle of doing theirjob.  Run with your OS-9 mounted as /h1, they find it and carry on,because SYS/login puts /h1/CMDS on PATH.  Run with only this disk,they stop at that point, and what you see is a program that startedand then quietly did nothing.The ones measured, with what each one wants:  pacman      `tmode', to set the terminal `nopause noecho' before it              draws.  Without it you get your name prompt, the              instructions question, one row of `+', and the end.  todos       `del' and `rename', to move its converted text over the  toos9       file you named.  Without them the conversion is still              made -- it waits under `todos.$$$.<n>' in the directory              you are standing in, and `mv' finishes the job.  creadoc     `dir' to list the .f files and `del' to clear its              temporary, both through a shell.  uuxqt       `procs', to see what is already running.  rayshade    a `shell' and a `cccp' in the data directory --              DOC/rayshade/README-RAYSHADE has the two copy lines.A program that forks a shell rather than a utility is a bigger groupand a different fix; DOC/README-SHELLS has it.How this list was made, in case you meet one it misses: `strings' overevery program under CMDS, looking for a bare utility name that is nota file on this disk.  It is a prompt rather than a proof -- `list',`free' and `link' are ordinary English words and turn up everywhere --so each of the five above was then run to see it stop.SIXTEEN PROGRAMS WANT A RAM DISK AT /r0---------------------------------------OS-9's C library makes a temporary file at /r0 -- the RAM disk a real systemhas -- and sixteen programs here carry that path inside them (every fileunder CMDS searched for it, 2026-09-23):  me  ed  hexed  bash  hist  roff  pri  preset  rndir  NEWS/rn  UUCP/mail  ELM/filter  GAMES/apfel  GAMES/graphsave  NETPBM/picttoppm  NETPBM/pnmsmoothOn your own OS-9 system /r0 is there and they work.  Under os9exec, makeone first:    mount -r=256k /r0-- the size on the option, the device as a path with its slash.  Withoutit a program that reaches for /r0 gets `/r0/ed.000003: 000:221 module notfound' and stops; `ed' and `hexed' fail outright, and the others only atthe moment they need the temporary file.WHERE THE PROGRAMS THINK THEY LIVE----------------------------------Some carry a hardcoded /h0 path.  That is not damage: they were built bypeople whose freeware lived on /h0.Most of those want just one file, /h0/sys/termcap -- and every one of themreads the TERMCAP environment variable first.  SYS/login sets it to thisdisk's own copy, so they work wherever the disk is mounted, with no /h0in sight.  That was measured, program by program, with no exceptions.What is left is a handful wanting data of their own under /h0, like/h0/games/hack/playground; mostly the gcc passes and the linker.  Goingthe other way, FAR MORE programs read their data from /dd --advent wants /dd/GAMES/adv/glorkz, fortune /dd/GAMES/FORTUNE/fortunes.dat,nroff /dd/LIB/tmac.*.The collection lives on /dd, and that is settled -- far more programs readtheir data from /dd than from /h0, nearly nine to one (479 against 55,measured 2026-09-19), so /dd is where it belongs.  DOC/DEPENDS lists every path each program opens.------------------------------------------------------------------THE ARRANGEMENT: collection on /dd and /h0, your OS-9 on /h1------------------------------------------------------------------On your own system: a descriptor named dd and one named h0, both reachingthe disk this collection is on -- which on most OS-9 systems is alreadyhow dd and h0 are related -- and your system disk under another name, /h1being the one SYS/login puts on the path.  Then    bash /dd/SYS/logingives a session with everything set, and the programs that want runb, yourshell, r68 or l68 find them on /h1.Under os9exec the same three devices, each named by an environmentvariable -- the image twice, once for each name it needs:    OS9DISK=<...>/osk-freeware.dd  OS9H0=<...>/osk-freeware.dd  \    OS9H1=<your OS-9>  os9exec -r bash /dd/SYS/loginKeep it as an RBF image.  os9exec can also mount a host directory as adevice, but file permissions and record locking work as OS-9 expects onlyon an image, and the emulator's own `mount -k' makes a blank one when youwant a disk of your own beside this one.Why each device:  /dd is where most programs read their data; /h0 is what a few carry  inside instead, and one disk under two names satisfies both.  Under  os9exec, do not write through both names at once in one session -- each  has its own sector cache, so a score written via /h0 need not show via  /dd until you remount.  /h1 is YOUR OS-9.  This collection leans on Microware pieces it does not  carry -- runb for the BASIC09 programs (bio, blackjack, wysetime), r68 and  l68 to finish a Fortran or assembly build, cc to compile, and Microware's  own shell for the programs that shell out.  SYS/login adds /h1/CMDS to PATH  and makes those resident from it, with the utilities programs here run by  name -- del, copy, tmode, pd and the rest; SYS/login lists them and says  which program wants each.  With no /h1 each load finds nothing and moves  on, so the collection still runs on its own -- /h1 adds your OS-9, it is  not required.The trailing /dd/SYS/login matters.  Started bare, bash has no PATH, HOME orTERM and finds no command; SYS/login exports those, sets TERMCAP to thisdisk's own termcap -- which is what takes the /h0-termcap programs down tothe few wanting data of their own -- and hands over to an interactive shell.Setting HOME is also what makes bash read /dd/.bashrc, where cd, pwd and thealiases are defined.Notes when running the disk this way:  - The image must stay writable.  hack, larn and cribbage write their    playgrounds and logs, and the score files ship with the disk.  - `sh', which the startup script names as its shell, starts but cannot    fork an external command by absolute path.  Use bash.  `ksh' can be the    first shell -- `os9exec -r /dd/CMDS/ksh' -- and forks external commands    normally; it reads no login script of its own, so set PATH yourself or    start it from bash.  - SIX NAMES BASH KEEPS FOR ITSELF: enable, fc, for, help, if and    trap are programs in CMDS and also bash builtins or keywords, so bash    answers first (`help' gives bash's list, `for' starts a loop).  Give the    path -- /dd/CMDS/help -- and they run.  DOC/STATUS lists what each is.------------------------------------------------------------------TAKING ONLY PART OF IT ONTO YOUR OWN MEDIA------------------------------------------------------------------You need not mount the whole image.  To move a few programs onto your owndisk, read DOC/DEPENDS first: it lists, per program, every absolute path thebinary carries and every file it opens by bare name.  Many programs needmore than their own binary -- a dictionary, a skeleton file, a scratchdirectory, a data tree under GAMES -- so copying just the command is oftennot enough.  `keep' (DOC/README-KEEP) copies a program with everythingDOC/DEPENDS lists for it.  Game data expects to sit under GAMES/, so copythat tree if you take the games.